// # DINNER.oot
//
// File: openOODA/DINNER.oot
// Purpose: spec for the academy-award .oo code style; canonical, long-form.
// Stage: governance
// Story: B-CODE-STYLE-V1; closes the engineering-doc gap flagged in W7 audit.
// Caps: (none)
//
// Logline: a header tells the story before the code earns the right to be read.
//
// Setup: GROK implements, META reviews, AGY reads for sanity; the user is
// out of the loop until the one-pass migration lands. Build path is broken;
// .oo files are source-of-truth text; .sh files remain runnable.
//
// Beats:
//   1. Why this style exists    2. Title block format    3. CAPs section
//   4. Tokenomics header        5. Script grammar        6. Linter rules
//   7. Phases                   8. Decisions (13)        9. Trade-offs
//  10. Rollback               11. Cross-references    12. What ships

// ## 1. Why this style exists

Code is told twice. Once to the machine, which forgives nothing. Once
to the human, who forgets everything. The header is the second telling.
It costs lines. It pays back in hours saved, bugs avoided, and onboarding
rounds skipped. Overhead is per file. Savings are per read. The read
happens more often than the write. Bet on the read.

256 lines per file is a constraint, not a prison. The linter is the law.

// ## 2. The title block — exact format

Every .oo file opens with five sections, in this order:

  // # <Title>                            ← one line, sentence case, no period
  //
  // File: <path relative to repo root>
  // Purpose: <one sentence, present tense, active voice>.
  // Stage: <lex|parse|tc|emit|cli|...>.
  // Story: <R1, M166, M-CTZ-1, ...> (optional).
  // Caps: <cap names> (optional).
  //
  // Logline: <one sentence, present tense, active voice, ends in a period>.
  //
  // Setup: <preconditions — what must be true before main runs>.
  //
  // Beats:
  //   1. <first beat>
  //   2. <second beat>
  //   3. <third beat>
  //
  // <optional: any other free-form comment lines>

Rules:
  Title is the function the file performs, not the file name.
  Logline is one sentence. If you need two, you have two functions.
  Beats list the story's plot points, not the code's line ranges.
  No marketing prose. No emoji. No "TODO" inside the header.
  Three beats is the floor. Six is the ceiling.

// ## 3. The CAPs section — exact format

A function that touches files, net, exec, env, time, rand, alloc,
threads, gpu, or ffi lists the caps it uses. Place the section between
the title block and the function body. Mirror the signature 1:1.

  // ## CAPs
  //
  //   <CapName>:    <one-line purpose>
  //
  //   (List only caps used in main or in private helpers called by main.)

Rules:
  One cap per line. Name first, colon, then one short purpose.
  Name matches CAPS.oot exactly. No aliases. Pick one and stick.
  Order: process, fs, env, net, time, rand, alloc, threads, gpu, ffi, other.
  Helpers called by main pull their caps through main's signature; helpers
  do not mint their own.
  Drift between signature and CAPs section is a build break.

Example A — single cap:
  pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap)
  // ## CAPs
  //
  //   ProcessCap:    run child commands

Example B — three caps:
  pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap, env: &EnvCap)
  // ## CAPs
  //
  //   ProcessCap:    run smoke
  //   FsReadCap:     load fixtures
  //   EnvCap:        read OODA_* keys

Example C — pure function:
  pub fn main()
  // ## CAPs
  //
  //   (none)

Common mistakes:
  Forgetting the section. Build passes the signature check, audit fails.
  Listing caps that aren't used. Audit flags a phantom token.
  Over-granting. SysCap where FsReadCap suffices. RULES 1.4.
  Naming caps inconsistently. fs vs fsRead vs FsRead. Compiler is case-sensitive.

// ## 4. The Tokenomics header — exact format

A canonical (long-form) file carries:

  // ## Tokenomics
  //
  //   canonical:    yes   # this is the long-form canonical version
  //   short-form:   <path-to-compact-form>  # optional, if it exists

A compressed short-form file carries:

  // ## Tokenomics
  //
  //   canonical:    no   # this file is the compressed form

Rules:
  canonical: yes means this file is the source of truth. Read it first.
  canonical: no means this file is itself the compressed form. The path
  to canonical lives in the short-form's title block, not here.
  Default to canonical. Reach for short-form only when tokens are the
  bottleneck (bulk migration, one-shot read).
  The short form is a child of the canonical. Never the other way around.

// ## 5. The story grammar for scripts

.oo scripts in ooda/scripts/*_smoke.oo use a small vocabulary:

  scenario(name)  — open a named story
  given           — setup precondition
  when            — begin the action
  and             — chain the next beat
  then            — the resolution
  expect_*        — assertion family (see ooda/std/scenario.oo)
  check           — a one-shot test

Form: title block, CAPs, then main reads top-to-bottom as a story.
`when` / `and` / `then` are the prose. `expect_*` and `check` are the work.
No `if r.is_err() { fail = 1; }` in main. Use the expect_* family.

Scripts must be .oo. Not .oos. Folder and name tell the job.
Canonical script standard: SCRIPTING.oot. Skeleton: ooda/scripts/script_template.oo.
Linter: ooda/scripts/script_lint.oo.

// ## 6. Rules the linter enforces

  - Title block present, in the exact five-section order.
  - Logline present, one sentence, ends in a period.
  - Beats present, numbered 1..N, three or more.
  - CAPs section present when the signature carries caps.
  - CAP names match CAPS.oot exactly (case-sensitive).
  - Tokenomics header present on every .oo file.
  - No `process_exec` direct calls outside main.
  - File line count <= 256.
  - No debug files, temp files, or secret keys in the tree.
  - No `.oos` extension. Scripts are `.oo`. Folder tells the job.

What the linter does not check (human responsibility):
  - Whether the logline is honest.
  - Whether the beats cover the real plot.
  - Whether a helper mints a cap its caller didn't grant.
  - Whether the short-form drifts from the canonical.

// ## 7. Phases (one-pass migration)

Phase A: Foundation (1 SWARM, 12 agents).
  - Write CODE_STYLE.oot, oodac/_template.oo, std/_template.oo, code_lint.oo.
  - Rewrite 6 of the BEST .oo files as proof-of-concept.
  - Write audit/code_style_rollup.oot.

Phase B: 5 worst files (5 SWARMs, 1 file each).
  - c_emit_helpers.oo (275 L), tc_call_arity.oo (230 L),
    parse_item.oo (243 L), voxels.oo (256 L), mtls_engine.oo (255 L).

Phase C: Sweep (4 SWARMs, ~1500 files).
  - C1: oodac/ files a-c. C2: d-l. C3: m-p. C4: r-w + std/.
  - Each agent processes 1-3 files; adds header + CAPs + Tokenomics + preludes.

Phase D: Cross-cutting (1 SWARM, 12 agents).
  - Update RULES.oot, SCRIPTING.oot, the 3 historical decision files.
  - Run linter on entire openOODA. Final rollup.

Phase E: Done.

// ## 8. Decisions (13 explicit)

 1. Vocabulary: `// # / // Purpose:` (NOT `// Logline:` — production code is not narrative).
 2. Priority: style guide first, then worst files, then sweep.
 3. Sprint tracking: new task (task 12).
 4. Scope: oodac/ + std/ + qa/tests/ (the core).
 5. Function naming: keep current verb-prefixed (`typecheck_*`, `c_emit_*`, `lex_*`).
 6. Doc on private fns: still required (`// ##` annotation).
 7. One-line vs two-line doc: prefer one-line unless non-obvious.
 8. Rewrite, not annotate: add the academy header directly; drop old `// job:`.
 9. Cross-cutting docs: apply new style to RULES.oot, BOOTSTRAP.oot, SPRINT.oot.
10. SWARM composition: GROK decides per phase. Each agent processes 1-3 files.
11. CAPs section: required for any file using caps. Machine-readable.
12. Tokenomics section: required for every file. Default canonical: yes.
13. 256 line max: strict. Any file >256 MUST be split. Linter enforces.

// ## 9. Trade-offs

Gain: every .oo file readable top-down; function names + preludes =
self-documenting; CAPs section is machine-readable for cap audits; Tokenomics
enables short-form loading for LLM context; linter enforces; migration is
mechanical.

Loss: ~1500 files need a header change (mechanical); some files may exceed
256 lines after adding preludes (e.g., c_emit_helpers.oo); agents may introduce
bugs in the worst files; "academy-award" is a strong word; some .oo files
will look forced.

// ## 10. Rollback

If the migration fails: `git revert <commit>` per Phase commit; sweep
re-runs until clean. The standards stay in CODE_STYLE.oot and SCRIPTING.oot
(no rollback). The reference files (CAPS, Tokenomics, Creative) stay
(no rollback). The templates stay (no rollback).

